TALLY_FINAL_MESSAGE={"verdict":"blocked","diagnosis":"Confirmed a real source defect in the committed build script, so checkpoint cp-proxy cannot be made to pass by anything inside its own lane: scripts/make-proxy.sh:310-316 defines read_header(path, parse) as `if parse is not None: return parse(path)`, returning the fork helper's value unchanged, but that helper is typed `parse_safetensors_header(path) -> tuple[dict, int]` and returns `(header, data_start)` (vllm/models/qwen4_exp/common/ple_mmap.py:128-173 in the pinned fork checkout, confirmed by reading the source; the campaign's own evidence note specs/flashnext/evidence/ple-54129.md:147-149 records the same two-value contract via `data_start + start`). main() binds `parse_header = getattr(ple_mmap, \"parse_safetensors_header\", None)` at scripts/make-proxy.sh:351, calls read_header at :364, and then iterates `for name, entry in header.items():` at :365 — which is line 239 of the embedded Python body (the `<<'PY'` heredoc opens at :126, so python line N maps to file line N+126), exactly the frame in the captured traceback, and the outer `<module>` frame at python line 406 is `sys.exit(main() or 0)` at :531-532. That produces precisely the reported `AttributeError: 'tuple' object has no attribute 'items'`. Notes that matter for the fix: the struct/json fallback branch at :314-316 returns a plain dict, so only the fork-present path is broken, and that is the only path taken inside the flashnext:dev container where the checkpoint runs; the fork parser already pops `__metadata__`, so the existing guard at :366-367 stays harmless after unwrapping. Why this is not a retry: cp-proxy is kind=checkpoint with a fixed worklist argv and its own brief states \"Do not modify the repository\", so no authorized edit exists in this lane — the diff for this attempt is empty (head == baseRev c06bb797203af3e2e4132de232c0f7d3d019d392), confirming nothing in the lane introduced or can remove the fault. scripts/make-proxy.sh and tests are the conflictDomains of the implementation task proxy-tooling, which is where the one-line unwrap belongs. Root cause of the escape: proxy-tooling's four acceptance criteria are `bash -n` plus greps (proxy-scripts-clean, proxy-serve-flags, proxy-checkpoint-layout) and a lane-clean check; handoff/ROUTING.md:38 itself calls this a \"weak grep gate\". tests/test_proxy_tooling.py extracts and imports the embedded body but never drives read_header against a stubbed two-value parser, so a return-type mismatch with the fork was structurally invisible until first light on the GPU. The amendment below fixes both the defect and the coverage hole; no secret-bearing values are involved.","proposal":{"kind":"amendment-task","paths":["scripts/make-proxy.sh","tests/test_proxy_tooling.py"],"goal":"Repair the safetensors header-parse return-type mismatch that fails checkpoint cp-proxy, inside proxy-tooling's existing ownership paths, and close the test hole that let it ship. (1) In scripts/make-proxy.sh, make read_header(path, parse) normalize the fork helper's result to the header dict: the fork's parse_safetensors_header is typed tuple[dict, int] and returns (header, data_start), so unwrap the first element when parse returns a tuple/list (keep it tolerant of a bare dict too, so the function is correct against both shapes), and leave the existing struct/json fallback branch returning a dict unchanged. Update the docstring to state that the function always returns the tensor-metadata dict regardless of which parser is used. Do not change any other behavior of the script: the workload mount stays read-only, no tensor data is read, the manifest's \"weight_bytes_read\": 0 invariant holds, and the script remains import-safe with all effectful steps under the __main__ guard. (2) In tests/test_proxy_tooling.py, add a unit test that imports the extracted builder body and calls read_header with a stub parse callable returning a two-tuple ({tensor_name: {\"dtype\": ..., \"shape\": [...], \"data_offsets\": [0, N]}}, 8), asserting the result is the dict and that iterating .items() on it succeeds; add a companion assertion that the parse=None fallback path still returns a dict for a minimal synthetic safetensors file written to a tempfile. This is the regression that would have caught the AttributeError without a GPU. (3) Commit on the assigned branch, touching only scripts/make-proxy.sh and tests/test_proxy_tooling.py, and leave git status --porcelain empty. Do not attempt the live GPU first light; cp-proxy re-executes make-proxy.sh and run-proxy.sh after this lands.","acceptanceCriteria":[{"id":"proxy-header-parse-returns-dict","description":"read_header normalizes the fork's tuple[dict, int] result to the header dict, and the parse=None fallback still yields a dict. Verified behaviorally by importing the extracted builder body, not by grep.","argv":["bash","-euc","python3 -m unittest tests.test_proxy_tooling -v 2>&1 | grep -qiE 'read_header|header_parse|header.*(tuple|dict)'"]},{"id":"proxy-tooling-suite-passes","description":"The full proxy tooling unit suite passes, including the new header-parse regression test.","argv":["bash","-euc","python3 -m unittest tests.test_proxy_tooling -v"]},{"id":"proxy-repo-suite-passes","description":"The repository-wide unittest discovery still passes, so the edit breaks no sibling lane's invariants.","argv":["bash","-euc","python3 -m unittest discover -s tests"]},{"id":"proxy-scripts-clean","description":"Both proxy scripts remain syntax-clean and run-proxy.sh still references the receipt path (unchanged from the original proxy-tooling gate).","argv":["bash","-euc","bash -n scripts/make-proxy.sh scripts/run-proxy.sh && grep -q 'results/receipts/proxy.json' scripts/run-proxy.sh"]},{"id":"proxy-embedded-python-compiles","description":"The embedded Python body of make-proxy.sh still compiles after the edit.","argv":["bash","-euc","python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport pathlib, re, py_compile, tempfile, os, sys\ntext = pathlib.Path('scripts/make-proxy.sh').read_text()\nbodies = re.findall(r\"<<'PY'[^\\n]*\\n(.*?)\\nPY\\n\", text, re.S)\nassert bodies, 'no embedded python body found in scripts/make-proxy.sh'\nfor i, b in enumerate(bodies):\n    p = os.path.join(tempfile.mkdtemp(), 'body%d.py' % i)\n    pathlib.Path(p).write_text(b)\n    py_compile.compile(p, doraise=True)\nprint('ok', len(bodies))\nEOF"]},{"id":"proxy-workload-read-only-invariant","description":"The read-only workload mount and the zero-weight-bytes manifest invariant are untouched by the fix.","argv":["bash","-euc","grep -q '\\-v \"$WORKLOAD_DIR:/workload:ro\"' scripts/make-proxy.sh && grep -q '\"weight_bytes_read\": 0' scripts/make-proxy.sh"]},{"id":"proxy-lane-clean","description":"The lane is fully committed: no uncommitted or untracked files remain after the task's commit.","argv":["bash","-euc","[ -z \"$(git status --porcelain)\" ]"]}],"dependencies":["proxy-tooling"]}}
